{#- modules/agents/running-loops.j2
    Where agent loops actually run, in practice.

    The rest of the deck argues that loops need verifiers. This slide is the
    unglamorous other half: something has to fire the loop on a schedule, and
    the answers people reach for are ordinary infrastructure rather than
    anything agent-specific. Deliberately a list of real options rather than
    a recommendation — the point is that the scheduler is a solved problem.

    Kept as a plain grid so entries can be added without reflowing anything.

    Written against the brand-pack primitive contract (see AGENTS.md): no hex
    colors, no brand class names. Import WITH CONTEXT:

      {%- from "modules/agents/running-loops.j2" import
          how_i_run_loops with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import
    content_slide,
    slide_heading,
    card_grid
-%}


{%- macro how_i_run_loops() -%}
{%- call content_slide("Running loops") %}
  {{ slide_heading("", "How I run loops today") }}
  {{ card_grid([
    {"title": "claude.ai routines",
     "body": "Scheduled Claude Code runs in the cloud. Nothing of mine has to be awake.",
     "accent": "primary"},
    {"title": "Claude Code <code>/schedule</code>",
     "body": "Cron for the session already in front of me.",
     "accent": "info"},
    {"title": "Host cron jobs",
     "body": "<code>crontab</code> on a machine I already operate. Unfashionable and completely reliable.",
     "accent": "secondary"},
    {"title": "EventBridge and Lambda",
     "body": "Serverless, and event-driven when a schedule is the wrong trigger.",
     "accent": "warning"},
    {"title": "Hermes / OpenClaw crons",
     "body": "Scheduled runs on the Hermes and OpenClaw stack.",
     "accent": "success"},
  ], cols=3) }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}
